as bootable drop-in: package cell state, receipt chain, interpreter ref, schema, public inputs? Maybe drop-in file format.\nP16 save organ bootable drop-in: manifest, cell files, organ boundary, exposed cells, deterministic replay.\nP17 save whole quilt bootable drop-in: self-contained image, boot loader reads manifest, reconstructs cells and chains.\nP18 nesting single cell in program: adapter exposes cell as reactive value; events feed receipts; failure if chain fork?\nP19 nesting organ: sub-quilt adapter with named exposed cells, versioned inputs/outputs, isolation.\nP20 nesting whole quilt: host quilt cells wire to sub-quilt exposed cells via input receipts; conflict namespace.\nP21 invariants common: chain hash continuity, before/after consistency, monotonic receipt sequence, no dangling parent hashes, deterministic replay.\nP22 failure modes common: hash chain breaks, partial receipt writes, missing predecessor, clock skew? Need concrete.\nP23 failure modes rewind/snapshot: nondeterministic inputs, external side effects, orphan references.\nP24 failure modes save/drop-in: version mismatch, non-replayable functions, large chains.\nP25 failure modes nesting: namespace collisions, provenance confusion, cross-quilt hash cycles.\nP26 bootable saved state validation and atomic deployment mechanism.\nP27 maybe costs/performance and garbage collection.\nNeed 22-30. Could be 27.\n\nNeed mechanism-first. Let's craft each point with exact fields, ledger rows, hashes, journal records, file formats.\n\nNeed maybe start with TRACE and numbered points. The answer summary <=200 words. The trace can include all.\n\nNeed ensure no meta-commentary. Self-contained. Use terms as defined.\n\nLet's design concrete system details.\n\nAssume receipt chain per cell:\nCell ID (organ/name), sequence number (per cell), prev_hash = SHA-256(prev receipt), before_image (value plus type/version), after_image, cause (input event id or dependency versions list), timestamp (logical clock), public key? maybe signature. Hash = SHA-256(seq || prev_hash || before || after || cause || timestamp). Receipt is journal record. Double-entry: every transition also records inverse receipt in an intent log? Hmm \"double-entry book-keeping\" maybe each receipt records before/after and previous hash. We can define.\n\nNeed maybe specific ledger:\n- Cell log file `cell.receipts` append-only line JSON.\n- Index `cell.idx` mapping sequence -> byte offset.\n- Chain tip in header.\n\nOrgans:\n- `organ.manifest` lists member cells, exposed cells, boundary, schema, version.\n- Organ transaction log to group multi-cell update: a group receipt or barrier marker. Could have \"organ journal\" with transaction id, member receipts, commit status.\n\nWhole quilt:\n- `quilt.manifest` lists organs, cells, top-level input journal.\n- Input journal `inputs.log` with event id, payload hash, logical timestamp.\n- Quilt header with root hash combining cell tips and input high-water mark.\n\nSnapshot:\n- consistent cut: stop writer, flush all receipts, record tips, copy state files and logs, write `snapshot.toml` with root hash.\n- For single cell: `cell_snapshot` = state value + chain tip receipt bytes + full chain? Need bootable: include chain for future rewind and provenance. Maybe snapshot includes full receipt chain up to tip (or at least back to last snapshot). Save as drop-in should include full chain or at least prefix? To be bootable and nest, need chain to verify and rewind. Could include complete receipt chain; has cost.\n\nRewind:\n- single cell: given target receipt hash or seq, verify chain from tip backward? Actually need find target, check hashes, then clone cell state to target's after/ before? Rewind means undo from tip to target. Mechanism: iterate from tip receipt to target, apply inverse of each after->before. Then truncate chain? Double-entry append a \"rewind receipt\" recording before current, after target, cause rewind_request. This preserves auditability; chain doesn't delete, it appends. But if rewind as time travel, chain tip moves backwards? We can choose append inverse receipts rather than truncate to keep hash chain. Need invariant maybe receipts monotonically append. For state rewind, current state equals target before_image; chain includes rollback receipts. But if saving after rewind, chain includes undo entries.\n- For organ: need all member cells rewound to target transaction boundary. Use organ cut hash or group receipt. Mechanism: group transaction id T, each member receipt references T and prev group hash. Rewind organ = for each cell in manifest, rewind to last receipt with transaction_id <= target; if one missing, abort.\n- Whole quilt: target quilt cut hash in quilt manifest. Rewind all cells to receipts with cuts <= target. Need input journal truncate? Appending inverse or rollback could be huge. Or snapshot restore.\n\nNeed failure modes:\n- Partial rewind due crash: some cells rewinded, some not -> organ violates atomicity; need prepare/commit journal with intent records and force flush before applying.\n- Chain hash mismatch: detect before applying each inverse.\n- Non-deterministic external call receipt cause has opaque input but replay sees different external state.\n- Concurrent writes during rewind/snapshot cause new receipts after copy; need write lock or version check on chain tip.\n\nSave as bootable drop-in: format maybe directory/zip with `quilt-image/`:\n- `manifest.json` / `quilt.toml`\n- `cells/<cell_id>.chain` full receipt log\n- `state/<cell_id>.value`\n- `organs/<organ_id>.json`\n- `inputs.log`\n- `interpreter.json` (runtime version, hashes)\n- `boot.lock` with root hash + signatures.\n\nMechanism: \"bootable drop-in\" means saved state can be loaded by host and run. Need describe boot sequence:\n1. Verify manifest hash against contents.\n2. Rebuild each cell's in-memory state from after_image of tip or replay full chain from genesis? If full chain included, replay deterministic functions; but stored state can be loaded and chain verified. Better: load state and verify chain tip matches manifest; do not replay if snapshots trusted.\n3. Install input hooks into host program; exposed cells mapped by manifest.\n4. Resume appending receipts with new host input cause.\n\nNeed invariants drop-in:\n- All receipt chains complete from genesis to tip? Actually include full chain and not compacted, or if compacted include a signed base snapshot and chain suffix.\n- Hash continuity from genesis to tip.\n- Exposed cell names unique per namespace.\n- Root hash includes interpreter version to prevent incompatibility.\n- No dangling references to host resources: save must materialize external inputs as sealed values or declared required ports.\n\nFailure modes save:\n- If save captures state but not final receipt due crash: tip mismatch; use atomic rename and fsync before manifest root hash.\n- If conversion functions non-replayable or native code pointers omitted, load fails.\n- Large log sizes; need snapshot compaction (checkpoint plus suffix) but must sign checkpoint.\n\nNesting:\n- Single cell drop-in into program: wrap cell in adaptor. Host writes input through a \"coupled receipt\" that references host event id and host value. The cell's chain continues. Failure: host causality not captured, double book-keeping diverges if same cell nested in two hosts (fork).\n- Organ nested as sub-quilt: host organ may expose only some cells; internal hidden cells are private; sub-quilt manifest is nested in host quilt with namespace prefix. Inputs to subquilt become receipts with cause `parent_cell` or `host_input`. Outputs read only exposed tip.\n- Whole quilt nested inside another quilt: parent quilt has a cell whose value is a \"sub-quilt handle\" or contains sub-quilt root hash. The child quilt's input journal is appended by parent event receipts; child's exposed outputs are read as dependency versions by parent cells. This creates cross-ledger references; avoid cycles by making child's receipts reference parent receipt hash but parent cell's receipt after_image includes only child output values, not hash? Need careful. If parent receipt references child output, child receipt references parent input, possible hash cycle. Mechanism: one-way causal arrow: parent input receipt is written to child input journal first with parent receipt hash; child computes outputs and appends receipts; parent then writes its final receipt referencing child output receipt hash. This forms DAG if parent final receipt occurs after child; no cycle? Child input receipt already contains parent input receipt hash; child output receipt contains child input hash; parent final contains child output hash. Parent input receipt doesn't contain parent final, no cycle. But if parent input and parent final are both parent receipts? Actually parent input writes an \"intent receipt\" in parent cell? Could maybe parent's current cell receipt before child has input pending. We can define phase protocol.\n\nNeed maybe include point on multi-quilt causal graph and cycle prevention. Good.\n\nNeed answer compact summary.\n\nNeed ensure each point has one idea and 2-6 sentences. The points should be concise but mechanism-first.\n\nLet's formulate.\n\nPotential outline:\n\nP1: Cell double-entry receipt structure (fields).\nP2: Hash chain continuity and append-only log.\nP3: Single-cell rewind mechanism.\nP4: Single-cell rewind invariants and failure.\nP5: Organ rewind mechanism using transaction receipts/cut.\nP6: Organ rewind invariants/failure.\nP7: Whole-quilt rewind mechanism using root cut and input journal high-water mark.\nP8: Whole-quilt rewind invariants/failure.\nP9: Single-cell snapshot mechanism.\nP10: Single-cell snapshot invariants/failure.\nP11: Organ snapshot mechanism: barrier and consistent cut manifest.\nP12: Organ snapshot invariants/failure.\nP13: Whole-quilt snapshot mechanism.\nP14: Whole-quilt snapshot invariants/failure.\nP15: Save-as-bootable-drop-in general format and single cell.\nP16: Organ bootable drop-in format.\nP17: Whole quilt bootable drop-in format and boot sequence.\nP18: Nesting single cell in host program.\nP19: Nesting organ as sub-quilt.\nP20: Nesting whole quilt in another quilt: causal protocol.\nP21: Common invariants across all.\nP22: Common failure modes (hash breaks, partial writes, divergence).\nP23: Rewind-specific failure modes.\nP24: Snapshot-specific failure modes.\nP25: Save/drop-in-specific failure modes.\nP26: Nesting-specific failure modes.\nP27: Verification/atomic deployment.\nP28: Costs and compaction.\n\nNeed count 28. Good.\n\nNeed each point 2-6 sentences. Need maybe P1 enough.\n\nLet's draft carefully with concrete fields. Ensure no references to conversation. Include definitions.\n\nNeed maybe \"receipt records...\" We'll mention hash algorithm. double entry: before and after images both present, plus inverse? They said double-entry book-keeping: every transition appends receipt to cell's hash-chained receipt log; receipt records what changed (before/after images), why, previous hash. Good.\n\nNeed maybe \"ledger rows\" as exact.\n\nLet's write.\n\nTRACE\nP1. The base unit is a cell receipt. Each receipt is an append-only JSON record: `{cell_id, seq, prev_hash, before, after, cause, logical_ts, receipt_hash}`. `before` and `after` are typed value images plus schema version; `cause` is either an input event id or the exact dependency versions observed; `receipt_hash = SHA-256(cell_id || seq || prev_hash || canon(before) || canon(after) || canon(cause) || logical_ts)`. This double-entry form keeps both images so reverse and replay do not need to re-execute effects to know what changed.\n\nP2. The ledger for a cell is stored as `cells/<cell_id>/receipts.log` with a sidecar `receipts.idx` mapping `seq -> byte_offset`, and a header file `tip.json` containing the last seq, last receipt_hash, and chain length. Append is implemented as write receipt bytes, fsync log, then atomically rewrite `tip.json` after fsync; the chain hash is the linkage: each receipt's `prev_hash` must equal the prior tip receipt_hash. The invariant is appending only never truncating, so rewinds and snapshots are represented as new records or sealed cuts, preserving provenance.\n\nP3. Single-cell rewind: given target `seq_t`, the runtime reads all receipts from `seq_t+1` to tip, verifies each `prev_hash` link and each receipt_hash. It then applies the inverse images in reverse order: for each receipt from tip down to `seq_t+1`, set state from that receipt's `before`. Instead of deleting, it appends a new \"rollback\" receipt at tip whose `before` is the current pre-rewind value, `after` is the target value, `cause` is `rewind_request(target_seq_t)`, and `prev_hash` is the old tip hash. The moved-back state is now the target value and the hash chain remains unbroken.\n\nP4. Single-cell rewind invariants: (a) the new chain tip must hash-cover the rollback receipt and all preceding receipts; (b) the restored value must equal the target receipt's `before`; (c) no receipt may be deleted. Failure modes: a crash midway leaves the old chain intact but no rollback receipt; a hash mismatch must stop before any inverse is applied; if two rollbacks race, the second uses the first's rollback as `prev_hash`, so they serialize but may create surprising audit history.\n\nP5. Organ rewind treats the organ as a write-boundary group. Each organ has `organs/<organ_id>/manifest.json` listing member cell ids and `group_receipts.log` where each multi-cell transition writes a group record `{group_txn_id, organ_id, members: {cell_id->receipt_hash_after}, prev_group_hash, group_hash}`. To rewind an organ to group transaction `T`, the runtime reads the group manifest, finds target group_receipt, and rewinds every member cell to the member receipt hash recorded under `T`. The organ journal then appends a group rollback record linking `prev_group_hash` to the last group hash and marking restored member hashes.\n\nP6. Organ rewind invariant: every member cell's tip after rewind must equal one of the hashes listed in the target group record, and no member may be missing; otherwise the organ is not a unit. Failure mode: if one member's chain is unavailable or corrupts, the group rewind cannot complete; partially rewinding some members during a crash breaks the group. Mitigation is a two-phase write: first append a prepared group rollback record, then apply member rewinds, then mark committed; on boot, a prepared-but-uncommitted record triggers completion or abort.\n\nP7. Whole-quilt rewind operates on a quilt cut. The quilt manifest contains `cells/` and `organs/`, an input journal `inputs.log` with entries `{input_id, payload_hash, logical_ts}`, and a root header `quilt.head` storing `root_hash = SHA-256(all cell tip hashes || all group tip hashes || input_high_water_mark)`. A whole-quilt rewind target is a prior root hash in `quilt.history`. The runtime maps that root to per-cell receipt hashes and input high-water mark, rewinds every cell to its recorded hash, truncates the live input view to the high-water mark by appending a quilt-rewind journal record, and writes a new root.\n\nP8. Whole-quilt rewind invariants: the target root must exist in `quilt.history`; every recorded cell hash must pass chain verification; after rewind, recomputed root must equal a new root with the rollback record included, not the old root. Failure mode: incomplete history missing per-cell hashes makes whole-quilt rewind impossible; cells added after the target root have no historical state and require a deletion or tombstone receipt; external input streams may have already advanced, so the input journal cannot silently drop events, it must append tombstones.\n\nP9. Single-cell snapshot takes a sealed cut without stopping the world. It writes `snapshots/cells/<cell_id>/<snapshot_id>.toml` containing the cell's current state image, tip seq, tip receipt_hash, schema version, and the full chain segment since the last snapshot or genesis. It then records a snapshot receipt in the live chain: `{before=current, after=current, cause=snapshot(snapshot_id), prev_hash=old_tip}` so the snapshot is tied to the ledger. The sidecar can later be used to restore quickly.\n\nP10. Single-cell snapshot invariants: snapshot state must equal the tip's `after`; snapshot hash must verify against contents; the snapshot receipt must be appended before any later transitions. Failure mode: if a transition occurs between reading state and tip, the snapshot can pair a stale state with a newer tip, violating the cut. The runtime must use a compare-and-swap on `tip.json` or hold the cell write lock from read to snapshot receipt.\n\nP11. Organ snapshot uses a barrier cut across all member cells. The organ journal appends `{barrier_id, organ_id, status=prepared}`; the runtime pauses writes to member cells, flushes all logs, reads each cell's tip and current value, writes `snapshots/organs/<organ_id>/<barrier_id>/cells/...` plus a manifest with member tip hashes, then appends `status=committed` with `organ_snapshot_hash`. This groups the cell states into one consistent cut because no member receipt may be appended between prepared and committed.\n\nP12. Organ snapshot invariants: the committed manifest hash must equal SHA-256 over all member snapshot files; each member tip at snapshot time must match its manifest entry; the organ group_tip must advance exactly once with the barrier commit. Failure mode: blocked writers or deadlock if a cell is also shared by another organ; crash after prepared leaves the organ paused until recovery sees the prepared record and either commits or rolls it back.\n\nP13. Whole-quilt snapshot is a global consistent cut. The runtime obtains the quilt write lock, flushes every cell log and organ journal, appends a `quilt_barrier` receipt to the input journal, reads all tips and state files, then writes `snapshots/quilt/<snapshot_id>/` with `quilt.head`, `cells/`, `organs/`, `inputs.log` up to the barrier, and `manifest.json`. The manifest stores the root hash, schema version, runtime version, snapshot timestamp, and file content hashes. The lock ensures no receipt interleaves across the cut.\n\nP14. Whole-quilt snapshot invariants: the root hash in the snapshot must equal the live recomputed root at the barrier; every file content hash must match its stored hash; the state and logs must be mutually consistent. Failure mode: if a sub-quilt nested inside the quilt writes asynchronously, a global lock must propagate to descendants or the snapshot may capture a child mid-transition. If files are copied without atomic snapshot directories, a crash can leave a truncated boot image.\n\nP15. Save-as-bootable-drop-in general mechanism: the saved artifact is a directory tree or single archive `quilt-dropin/<name>/` with `manifest.json`, `boot.lock`, `cells/<cell_id>/receipts.log`, `cells/<cell_id>/tip.json`, `state/<cell_id>.value`, `organs/<id>/manifest.json`, `inputs.log`, and `runtime.json`. `manifest.json` lists exposed cells and organs, content SHA-256 hashes, version, and root hash. For a single cell, the package includes that one cell's full receipt chain, current state, schema, and a port declaration `{name, direction, type}` so a host program can attach it.\n\nP16. Organ bootable drop-in: the package includes the organ manifest, its member cell chains and states, the organ group journal, and only the exposed cell ports are declared in `ports.json`; private member cells are not exposed but are saved. The loaded organ is isolated: host calls enter through exposed port cells, and internals only change via its own receipts. The saved organ must carry its full group history up to the saved tip so later group rewinds and audits remain valid.\n\nP17. Whole-quilt bootable drop-in: the saved image is a full quilt with all cells, organs, input journal, root history, and a boot loader that loads `manifest.json`, verifies all file hashes, restores each cell state from `state/` and checks `tip.json` linkage, verifies the root hash, then starts accepting inputs in the new host. Boot sequence is atomic: the image has a sealed `boot.lock` containing the expected manifest hash; if any file hash mismatches, boot fails without mutating any ledger.\n\nP18. Nesting a single cell drop-in into a program: the host creates a wrapper object that holds an in-memory pointer to the cell, exposes the current `after` value, and writes a new receipt for every host write. The host write receipt uses `cause = {host_event_id, host_payload_hash}` and `before` as the previous value; the cell remains a complete reactive unit. Failure mode: if the same cell drop-in is opened twice, two live copies share the same chain name but not the same ledgers, causing forks; this must be rejected by a lease file or instance id in the drop-in.\n\nP19. Nesting an organ as a sub-quilt: the parent quilt records the child organ under a namespace, e.g. `sub.<organ_name>.<cell_name>`. Parent inputs are written as receipts into the child's exposed input cells with `cause` referencing the parent input event, and parent reads from child output cells using the child's tip hashes as dependency versions. The organ's private cells are invisible; all cross-boundary value flow is through declared ports.\n\nP20. Nesting a whole quilt inside another quilt requires a causal protocol that prevents hash cycles. The parent first writes its own input receipt storing the intended child input value and parent event id. That receipt is then posted to the child's input journal as a child input event with `cause = parent_receipt_hash`. The child runs and appends its output receipts. Then the parent writes its final receipt whose `after` incorporates the child output value and whose `cause` includes the child output receipt hashes. This forms a DAG: parent-input -> child-input -> child-output -> parent-final; no hash cycle exists.\n\nP21. Invariants across all operations: (a) every receipt_hash equals the canonical hash of its own fields; (b) every `prev_hash` points to the preceding receipt exactly; (c) before/after images are type-valid and schema-versioned; (d) each cell has one tip at any moment under a write lock; (e) every saved cut/root is content-addressed and reproducible from its files; (f) append-only ledgers mean rewind/snapshot do not erase history. These invariants can be checked by a verifier scanning the receipt chains and manifests.\n\nP22. Common failure modes: partial appends leave a receipt in the log but no updated tip file; recovery must scan log tail, verify the last complete receipt, and rebuild the tip. Hash chain breaks reveal corruption or tampering and must halt before applying changes. Clock skew in logical timestamps matters less if every receipt has a monotonic per-cell `seq`, but whole-quilt ordering across cells uses the group/root barriers, not wall-clock time. Missing or duplicate `seq` numbers violate ledgers and must be detected by index rebuild.\n\nP23. Rewind-specific failure modes beyond broken chains: a rewind target receipt may depend on external inputs that no longer exist; reapplying inverse images is safe for pure state but not for external resources like files, network messages, or hardware handles, because those are not inside the before/after images. Whole-quilt rewind can resurrect deleted cells or organs; tombstones are needed. Organ rewind can fail atomically if any member is on a fork.\n\nP24. Snapshot-specific failure modes: non-atomic copying of many files can capture a mixed cut even when receipts are flushed, especially with nested sub-quilts; use atomic directory switch and propagate barriers. Snapshot accumulation bloats the log if every snapshot appends a snapshot receipt and archives whole chains. The snapshot receipt's `cause=snapshot` is not a normal input, so tools must know to ignore it for dependency semantics.\n\nP25. Save/drop-in-specific failure modes: the saved image may reference runtime version, native code, or external services that are unavailable in the new program; the manifest must record exact runtime and dependency hashes, and boot must fail if incompatible. A saved organ may have hidden internal input ports that are required but absent in the new host; the required ports list must be explicit. Serializing full chains can leak sensitive historical data and grow large; snapshots can compact the chain behind a signed checkpoint, but compaction changes provenance.\n\nP26. Nesting-specific failure modes: namespace collisions occur when two nested quilts expose the same cell name; manifests must use fully qualified names and reject duplicate ports. Cross-quilt provenance confusion arises if a host cell records a child output hash in its cause but the child later rewinds or forks; the host can read an output that no longer exists. Shared cells between organs can produce concurrent receipts from two group journals, requiring an ownership rule: a cell belongs to at most one organ unless explicitly exported as multi-organ with a shared lock.\n\nP27. Verification and atomic deployment: before any saved drop-in is booted, a verifier recomputes all file hashes, each cell chain from its genesis/tip, each organ group hash, and the final root hash; it also checks that every exposed port name is unique and typed. Deployment uses a staging directory and atomic rename; the host obtains a lease/lock on the target namespace. After boot, the first new receipt must use the saved tip as `prev_hash`, otherwise the saved state is not a live continuation.\n\nP28. Costs and compaction: retaining full receipt chains from genesis gives rewind and audit but costs unbounded storage and makes drop-ins large. A practical system stores signed compacted checkpoints: a checkpoint file includes a base state image and the hash of the last omitted receipt, and the live chain continues from that hash. The invariants remain if the checkpoint is content-hashed and referenced as the genesis for future verifications; failure mode is that omitting history makes rewinds before the checkpoint impossible or requires a full snapshot.\n\nNeed maybe check count 28 points. Good. Need maybe include \"How should use cells' double-entry book-keeping\" answer. We did.\n\nNeed final ANSWER <=200 words. Need compact summary. Include mechanisms, invariants, failure modes. Need maybe 5-7 sentences. Let's craft.\n\nANSWER:\nA quilt should treat receipt chains as append-only hash-linked ledgers; rewind moves state backward by appending rollback receipts, snapshot records consistent cuts and bars across cells/organs, and save packages the chains, state, cut roots, ports, and runtime version into a verified drop-in archive. Per cell, rewind/snapshot use `seq`, `prev_hash`, before/after images, tip file compare-and-swap. Organs add group transaction/barrier records naming member receipt hashes. Whole quilts use a root hash over all cell tips, organ tips, and input journal high-water mark, with global barriers. Boot verifies all file hashes and chain links before resuming. Key invariants: every `prev_hash` links, receipt_hash covers fields, one tip per cell, group/root cuts are content-addressed, ledgers append only. Main failure modes: partial writes or crashes, hash breaks, non-atomic cuts, missing external inputs, version mismatch, namespace collisions, and unbounded chain growth. Fixes: two-phase prepare/commit, atomic directory switch, fsync ordering, explicit ports, leases, and signed compacted checkpoints. That summary likely >200? Need count. Let's count words roughly: 167? Need ensure <=200. It is maybe 180. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include no meta. Fine.\n\nLet's ensure the trace points aren't too long. Some points maybe 6 sentences. Need okay.\n\nNeed maybe ensure in P1 \"double-entry book-keeping\" explicitly. P1 names fields. Good.\n\nNeed maybe maybe \"receipt chain\" not \"blockchain\", but hash chained. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"invariants, failure modes\" in each granularity for rewind snapshot save nesting. The user asks \"Specify the mechanisms, invariants, and failure modes.\" It might want for each operation/granularity? Our points cover mechanism + invariant/failure per granularity for rewind and snapshot; for save/drop-in and nesting we cover mechanism and failure. Need maybe be explicit with invariants for save/nesting as well. Some points P15/P16/P17 maybe mechanisms; P21 common invariants; P26 failure. Could be enough. But to satisfy \"over three asked dimensions — mechanisms, invariants, failure modes — for each of: rewind, snapshot, save-as-bootable-drop-in, and nesting; at each of three granularities.\" Maybe we need ensure for save and nesting at each granularity, there are invariants and failure modes in same points. Could add a few more points or adjust.\n\nMaybe our trace is mechanism heavy but invariants/failure for save maybe P15 no failure; P16 no; P17 no. P25 has save/drop-in failures; P21 common invariants includes saved cut. Could be acceptable. But maybe we should include explicit invariants for save at each granularity and nesting at each granularity. We have P15-P17 mechanisms for save; P25 failure modes; P21 invariants. Could maybe make P15 include single-cell save invariant and failure. P16 organ save invariant and failure. P17 whole quilt save invariant and failure. Then P25 maybe excess. Let's revise points to ensure.\n\nNeed maybe rewrite P15 to include mechanism and invariant/failure for single cell. P16 mechanism and invariant/failure for organ. P17 mechanism and invariant/failure whole. P18 mechanism invariant/failure single nesting. P19 organ nesting invariant/failure. P20 whole nesting invariant/failure. That would satisfy. But we also need 28 points. We can use P15-P20 for that; P21-P28 common. Let's adjust to avoid redundancy.\n\nLet's refine P15:\nP15 single-cell drop-in save: package file `cells/<cell_id>/receipts.log` full chain, `state/<cell_id>.value`, `tip.json`, `port.json`. Manifest includes content hashes. Invariant: saved state equals tip `after`, chain tip in manifest equals tip on disk. Failure: full chain absent or compacted unsafely -> boot can't verify; copy after a concurrent write -> mismatch.\n\nP16 organ drop-in save: manifest with member list, ports, group journal, member chains, `organ_snapshot_hash`. Invariant: all member tip hashes in organ snapshot committed record. Failure: private member omitted or port names duplicate; group journal partial.\n\nP17 whole quilt drop-in save: root hash, file hashes, runtime ref, etc. Invariant: root hash recompute equals manifest. Failure: runtime/native mismatch, missing input journal, partial copy.\n\nP18 single nesting: mechanism. invariant: host writes are receipts with `prev_hash`, no fork. Failure: concurrent instance fork.\n\nP19 organ nesting: mechanism. invariant: boundary only through ports, child organ's internal chain remains self-contained. Failure: port mismatch/unexposed hidden dependencies.\n\nP20 whole quilt nesting: mechanism. invariant: causal DAG no hash cycles, parent-child references are one-way at each transaction. Failure: cycle if host writes parent final before child input? Actually create cycle.\n\nThen P21-P28.\n\nNeed maybe P13/P14 whole snapshot, P9/P10 etc already. That's okay.\n\nLet's count if we adjust P15-P20 to include all three dimensions: yes.\n\nNeed maybe P3/P4 single rewind; P5/P6 organ rewind; P7/P8 whole rewind. P9/P10 single snapshot; P11/P12 organ snapshot; P13/P14 whole snapshot. P15/P16/P17 save at three. P18/P19/P20 nesting. That's 20 points. Remaining P21-P28 common invariants/failures/costs. Good.\n\nNeed maybe make each point not too long. The final output desired oververbosity 5 but user specified extended 22-30 points. We'll write concise per point. Need ensure points are each 2-6 sentences. Some maybe 3 sentences. Good.\n\nLet's generate final. Need no markdown beyond heading. Use \"P1.\" etc. Then ANSWER. Make sure no analysis. final with TRACE.\n\nNeed maybe use exactly \"TRACE\" heading line. Then points. Then \"ANSWER\". Let's craft final carefully.\n\nDouble-check possible issues:\n- \"budget guard: trace must fit; be concise per point.\" We are okay.\n- \"SELF-CONTAINED: no references to any conversation\" okay.\n- \"Format: heading line TRACE, then numbered points P1..Pn, then heading line ANSWER with compact summary (<=200 words).\" We'll do.\n\nLet's produce final.",
